{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$ \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 7=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-14)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 7=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-3)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-14)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 7=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-14)=0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  \"${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a49f786d17f3e4eae8c724ace1d18cbd9300e626881f526d3ee8de5189aa92e4"} {"question" "q-39950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ \nSo, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a49f786d17f3e4eae8c724ace1d18cbd9300e626881f526d3ee8de5189aa92e4"} {"question" "q-39953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-39953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-80539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-80539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=5$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{-2+5}{-1}=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-80539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a49f786d17f3e4eae8c724ace1d18cbd9300e626881f526d3ee8de5189aa92e4"} {"question" "q-80539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-80539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-80539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & \\frac{7}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=6,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=15,$ and $c=6.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{15+6}{1}=21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a49f786d17f3e4eae8c724ace1d18cbd9300e626881f526d3ee8de5189aa92e4"} {"question" "q-140466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-9"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{10}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =9\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5+4\\cdot 7=36\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =9\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =9\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5+4\\cdot 7=24\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{2}$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a49f786d17f3e4eae8c724ace1d18cbd9300e626881f526d3ee8de5189aa92e4"} {"question" "q-140467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" "62abc23251d661affa4f4b923050f838d562d7cc2673850904cb0a2929efaf40"} {"question" "q-140467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074723/state.json" "file_sha256" 